\begin{abstract}
We present a multi-scale computational framework for integrating non-linear astronomical dynamical systems over secular timescales. The architecture combines a time-reversible 4th-order Yoshida symplectic integrator with First Post-Newtonian (1PN) relativistic metric corrections, zero-trust cryptographic dataset envelopes (AES-256-GCM and Kyber-1024), and an autonomous diagnostic engine for real-time stability assessment. Dynamic classification is achieved using full 2N Lyapunov spectrum evaluation via continuous QR decomposition, Mean Exponential Growth Factor of Nearby Orbits (MEGNO), Frequency Map Analysis (FMA), and Chirikov resonance overlap criteria. We demonstrate multi-cluster federation scaling using Karmada and Ray across heterogeneous GPU clusters, enabling high-resolution 3D chaos atlas parameter sweeps.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Modeling long-term dynamic stability in planetary and astrophysical systems requires numerical methods that strictly preserve phase-space volume. Classical non-symplectic integrators accumulate secular energy drift over extended timescales, obscuring genuine dynamic phenomena such as KAM torus breakdown and Arnold diffusion.

This work details an end-to-end software architecture that enforces time-reversibility, zero-trust dataset integrity, and federated parameter scanning for high-dimensional stability classification.

\section{Governing Mathematical Physics}
\subsection{1PN Relativistic Hamiltonian Equations}
For an $N$-body gravitational system with masses $m_i$, positions $\mathbf{r}_i$, and velocities $\mathbf{v}_i$, the 1PN relativistic acceleration is given by:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{a}_i = \sum_{j \neq i} G m_j \frac{\mathbf{r}_j - \mathbf{r}_i}{r_{ij}^3} \left[ 1 - \frac{4}{c^2} \sum_{k \neq i} \frac{G m_k}{r_{ik}} + \left(\frac{v_i}{c}\right)^2 + 2\left(\frac{v_j}{c}\right)^2 - \frac{4}{c^2}(\mathbf{v}_i \cdot \mathbf{v}_j) \right]
\end{equation}

\subsection{Symplectic Phase-Space Preservation}
Phase-space volume is preserved by applying the 4th-order Yoshida operator split $S_{\Delta t} = \prod_{k=1}^4 e^{c_k \Delta t A} e^{d_k \Delta t B}$, where:
\begin{equation}
w_0 = -\frac{2^{1/3}}{2 - 2^{1/3}}, \quad w_1 = \frac{1}{2 - 2^{1/3}}
\end{equation}

\section{Chaos Diagnostics \& KAM Stability}
\subsection{MEGNO and Frequency Map Analysis}
The continuous MEGNO indicator $\langle Y(t) \rangle$ is evaluated alongside the trajectory flow:
\begin{equation}
\langle Y(t) \rangle = \frac{2}{t} \int_0^t \frac{\|\dot{\mathbf{\delta w}}(s)\|}{\|\mathbf{\delta w}(s)\|} s \, ds
\end{equation}
For quasi-periodic trajectories on invariant KAM tori, $\lim_{t \to \infty} \langle Y(t) \rangle = 2.0$.

